WEBVTT

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:01.979
Hi, I'm Jackstraw!

00:00:01.979 --> 00:00:04.936
Molecular dynamics crawls one femtosecond at a time.

00:00:04.936 --> 00:00:08.262
Here's how a network learns steps ten times bigger.

00:00:10.183 --> 00:00:11.568
Don't just learn the force.

00:00:11.568 --> 00:00:14.892
Learn where the molecule ends up after a big step, given the

00:00:14.892 --> 00:00:16.276
random kicks along the way.

00:00:19.692 --> 00:00:22.415
The update looks like the simplest integrator there is.

00:00:22.835 --> 00:00:26.272
But the force is averaged along the true path, not taken at the start.

00:00:26.692 --> 00:00:28.925
The velocity is averaged over the step too.

00:00:29.544 --> 00:00:32.294
And the thermal kick is sampled first and handed in.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:37.714
A transformer reads the atoms, their velocities, and the step size.

00:00:38.134 --> 00:00:42.420
It also reads four polynomial coefficients per atom that summarise the Brownian

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:43.848
path over the step.

00:00:44.268 --> 00:00:47.613
Two heads predict the averaged velocity and the averaged force.

00:00:48.033 --> 00:00:51.754
One update jumps to the next state, up to ten femtoseconds later.

00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:54.974
No trajectories needed.

00:00:55.683 --> 00:00:57.794
Take a single conformer and its forces.

00:00:58.837 --> 00:01:01.099
Draw velocities at the target temperature.

00:01:01.768 --> 00:01:04.654
At a zero step, the force head must match the true force.

00:01:05.074 --> 00:01:07.836
And one big step must land where two half steps land,

00:01:07.836 --> 00:01:08.841
on the same path.

00:01:10.383 --> 00:01:13.517
A smooth polynomial summarises the grey Brownian path.

00:01:13.937 --> 00:01:17.720
From it, the flow map jumps velocity and position onto the fine path.

00:01:19.692 --> 00:01:22.981
The usual integrator: about one femtosecond per force call.

00:01:23.865 --> 00:01:27.057
The flow map: up to ten, but it needs a temperature corrector.

00:01:29.883 --> 00:01:34.006
On alanine dipeptide, that is ten times the same network as a

00:01:34.006 --> 00:01:36.411
force field, and level with classical dynamics.

00:01:37.808 --> 00:01:40.411
Learning the integral of the forces buys bigger steps.

00:01:40.411 --> 00:01:43.014
The sampler is only as good as the fit.

00:01:45.775 --> 00:01:49.152
Average over the step, hand in the noise, and train on half steps.

00:01:50.375 --> 00:01:52.493
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:52.493 --> 00:01:53.098
I'm Jackstraw.

00:01:53.098 --> 00:01:53.400
Bye!
